> Basic Navigation in the Terminal

SNAPS-OS comes pre-configured with eza, zoxide and fzf to help with navigation.

eza and zoxide replace ls and cd respectively. fzf gives fuzzy finding integration.

when you spawn a terminal, you'll see something like:

     ~

this is prettified using powerlevel10k, and shows your *current* directory.

you can also get the current directory using the command `pwd` (print working directory)

by default, this is your home directory, which has `~` as a shorthand.
the longform is /home/yourusername.

some scripts and commands won't like using ~, so you need to use the full path
(for example, this is important with the `ln` command for symlinks)
in this case, use $(whoami) for username-agnostic paths.

to list files in the current directory, type `ls`

this uses eza under the hood and gives you a line-by-line output with icons.

the lines will look something like:
drwxr-xr-x     - den 24 may 23:57   test

here's what each thing means:
drwxr-xr-x - permissions string
- - file size (not shown for directories)
den - the account that owns the file
24 may 23:57 - last modified time
name - the filename with icon

symlinks appear as:
lrwxrwxrwx     - den 12 apr 05:04   .vscode -> /home/den/.vscode-oss

to move into a directory, use `cd <dirname>`

cd uses zoxide under the hood, which gives fuzzy finding for visited directories.
instead of `cd /home/den/ownCloud/ArchConfig/MangoScripts`, you can run `cd mangoscripts`
if that's the best match for what you've visited.

path syntax:
~ - shorthand for /home/yourusername
. - current working directory
.. - one directory up
... - two directories up
.... - three directories up

examples:
`cd dir` - relative to current directory
`cd ~/dir` - absolute from home
`cd ./dir` - explicit current dir
`cd /dir` - absolute from root
`cd ../dir` - one up, then into dir
`cd sub` - zoxide fuzzy match
